---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';
import { APP, AUTHOR, REPO } from '../lib/site';

/** Kept in one place: the date this text last changed, not the build date. */
const UPDATED = '16 August 2026';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Privacy — ipnx"
  description="ipnx collects nothing. No accounts, no analytics, no telemetry, no crash reporting. This page explains exactly what the app does and does not do with your data."
  eyebrow="Legal"
  heading="Privacy"
  intro="ipnx collects nothing about you. This page says so precisely, including the parts a shorter policy would leave out."
>
  <Prose>
    <p><strong>Last updated:</strong> {UPDATED}</p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
      {APP.name} has no accounts, no analytics, no telemetry, no crash reporting and no
      advertising. It does not contact any server belonging to this project, because there is no
      such server. Nothing you type into the emulated machine, and nothing about how you use the
      app, is transmitted anywhere or seen by anyone.
    </p>

    <h2>What is stored, and where</h2>
    <p>
      Everything {APP.name} keeps lives on your Mac, in your own account, and is removed when you
      delete the app and its support folder:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The emulated machine's disk</strong> —
        <code>~/Library/Application Support/ipnx/v8/v8.disk</code>. Its contents are whatever you
        do inside the emulated Unix: files you create, commands you run, mail you send to
        yourself. It is an ordinary file and you can copy, export or delete it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A snapshot of the running machine</strong> — <code>state.sav</code>, written when
        you quit so the session can resume where it stopped.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Terminal contents</strong> — what was last on each screen, so a resumed session
        looks like the one you left.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your settings</strong> — screen size, phosphor colour, scaling and similar, in the
        standard macOS preferences for the app.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A bookmark to any folder you choose to share</strong>, so the choice survives a
        relaunch. The bookmark records which folder; the folder's contents are not copied
        anywhere.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The network, stated honestly</h2>
    <p>
      This is the part a boilerplate policy would skip, and it is the part most worth being
      straight about. {APP.name} contains a complete 1985 TCP/IP implementation, running inside
      the emulated machine, and the app gives it a way out to the real Internet through a
      user-mode network translator. That means:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The app itself never initiates a network connection.</strong> It has no
        update check, no beacon, no first-run ping.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The emulated machine can, if you tell it to.</strong> If you run a command in
        Eighth Edition that resolves a name or opens a connection, that traffic leaves your Mac
        exactly as any other program's would, to whoever you directed it at. It is your traffic,
        under your control, and this project neither sees nor records it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Name lookups follow your Mac.</strong> The emulated machine asks the network
        translator, which asks whatever resolver macOS is already using.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>You can switch the card off.</strong> Settings → Machine → Ethernet card removes
        the network hardware from the emulated computer entirely, and it then boots as a machine
        with no networking at all.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Folders you share</h2>
    <p>
      {APP.name} can mount a folder of your choosing inside the emulated machine, at
      <code>/n/macos</code>, and your home folder at <code>/n/home</code>. This is served in-process
      over the local loopback interface — it never leaves your Mac and is not reachable from
      another computer. The emulated machine gets read-only access unless you explicitly allow
      writing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Choose deliberately. A folder you share is a folder the emulated Unix can read, and a
      1985 system has 1985 ideas about file names and permissions.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      {APP.name} is not directed at children, collects nothing from anyone, and contains no
      user-generated content, purchases or communication features.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, the date above changes with it and the previous wording remains in
      the <a href={REPO}>repository's</a> history — there is no version of it you cannot go back
      and read.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about this policy, or about anything the app does: <a href={`mailto:${AUTHOR.email}`}
        >{AUTHOR.email}</a
      >. See also <a href={href('/support')}>support</a>.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</PageLayout>
